The classic SAS Data Step
Consider a common scenario: we have a dataset of patient weights baseline and week 12, and we want to calculate the percentage change from baseline, but only for patients in the active treatment arm.
In SAS, you might write something like this:
/* SAS Code */
data weight_change;
  set adsl;
  where arm = 'Active';
  pct_change = ((weight_w12 - weight_bl) / weight_bl) * 100;

  if pct_change &gt;= 5 then flag = 'Y';
  else flag = 'N';
run;
It’s beautiful in its simplicity. You read a record, filter it, calculate a new variable, assign a flag, and output.

What about FIRST. and LAST. processing?
This is where SAS programmers often panic. If there’s no PDV sequentially reading rows, how do we identify the first or last record of a patient?
In R, we solve this with grouping.
Let’s say we want to find the maximum weight for each subject across multiple visits.
/* SAS Code */
proc sort data=weights;
  by usubjid descending weight;
run;

data max_weight;
  set weights;
  by usubjid;
  if first.usubjid;
run;
In R, {dplyr} does this incredibly cleanly:

# Mock Weights Data
weights &lt;- data.frame(
  usubjid = c("001", "001", "002", "002", "002"),
  visit = c("Baseline", "Week 4", "Baseline", "Week 4", "Week 8"),
  weight = c(80, 81, 75, 74, 73)
)

# R Code using dplyr
max_weight &lt;- weights %&gt;%
  group_by(usubjid) %&gt;%
  slice_max(weight, n = 1, with_ties = FALSE) %&gt;%
  ungroup()

Or similarly, just arranging and slicing:

max_weight2 &lt;- weights %&gt;%
  arrange(usubjid, desc(weight)) %&gt;%
  group_by(usubjid) %&gt;%
  slice(1) %&gt;%
  ungroup()

By grouping the data frame, {dplyr} verbs suddenly operate within those groups. We don’t need to manually check first.usubjid—we just tell R to grab the first row (slice(1)) or the maximum value (slice_max) for each group.

1. Procedures vs. Functions
In SAS, you think in Procedures (PROCs). You set up a procedure, give it options, and run it.
/* SAS: Procedure-based */
proc means data=sashelp.class mean std;
  var height weight;
run;
In R, you think in Functions. You pass data into a function, and it returns a result. You often chain these functions together.
# R: Function-based
library(dplyr)

sashelp_class %&gt;%
  summarise(
    mean_height = mean(height),
    sd_height = sd(height)
  )

2. The Implicit Loop vs. Vectorization
The SAS DATA step has an implicit loop. It processes row 1, then row 2, etc.
data new;
  set old;
  if age &gt; 18 then group = 'Adult';
  else group = 'Pediatric';
run;
In R, operations are vectorized. The ifelse function checks the entire age column at once and returns a new column.
# R: Vectorized
new_data &lt;- old_data %&gt;%
  mutate(group = if_else(age &gt; 18, "Adult", "Pediatric"))

In SAS, macros are text substitution. They are powerful but hard to debug. In R, you write Functions. Functions are first-class citizens. They accept objects as input and return objects.


SAS Macro:
%macro print_stats(dset, var);
  proc means data=&dset;
    var &var;
  run;
%mend;
R Function:
print_stats &lt;- function(df, col_name) {
  df %&gt;%
    summarise(
      mean_val = mean(.data[[col_name]], na.rm = TRUE),
      sd_val = sd(.data[[col_name]], na.rm = TRUE)
    )
}

print_stats(dm, "AGE")
Note: The .data[[col_name]] syntax is needed for “Tidy Evaluation” when passing column names as strings.

