1. Stop Using For-Loops!
If you write a for(i in 1:nrow(df)) loop in R to calculate a value, you are doing it wrong.
In R, vectors are processed natively in C behind the scenes. When you loop over a vector element by element, R has to evaluate the code environment and dispatch the function on every single iteration. This causes massive overhead.
Bad (Row-by-Row Iteration):

# Example: Categorizing Ages
adsl$AGEGR1 &lt;- character(nrow(adsl))

for (i in seq_len(nrow(adsl))) {
  if (adsl$AGE[i] &lt; 65) {
    adsl$AGEGR1[i] &lt;- "&lt;65"
  } else {
    adsl$AGEGR1[i] &lt;- "&gt;=65"
  }
}

Good (Vectorization):

library(dplyr)

# R applies this completely in parallel (vectorized)
adsl &lt;- adsl %&gt;%
  mutate(AGEGR1 = if_else(AGE &lt; 65, "&lt;65", "&gt;=65"))

The vectorized {dplyr} code above is not just cleaner; it runs literally thousands of times faster on massive datasets.

The Magic of {teal}
With just 30 lines of code, you have launched a professional web application.
Here is what the medical reviewer gets when they open the app: 1. The Filter Panel: On the right side, {teal} automatically generates a filter panel reading your dataset. The user can filter ADSL by AGE &gt; 65 or filter ADAE by AESEV == "SEVERE". The tables instantly update. 2. Dynamic UI: In the “Demographic Table” tab, the user can click dropdowns to change the grouping variable from ARM to SEX, and add BMI to the summary statistics. 3. Show R Code: Most importantly for validation, {teal} includes a “Show R Code” button. When the medical writer configures the exact table they want, they click this button, and {teal} provides the exact {rtables} R code necessary to perfectly reproduce that specific table in a validated script.

The Core Concept of {data.table}
The {dplyr} package creates a completely fresh copy of a column whenever you modify it (mutate). The {data.table} package prefers to modify in place by reference. This means it doesn’t copy the data in RAM—it just reaches into memory and overwrites the exact bytes you asked it to.
This leads to staggering performance improvements and virtually zero RAM spikes.
